%I A401192 #7 Oct 24 2026 17:11:05
%S A401192 1,1,1,2,1,2,3,1,3,4,2,4,3,2,5,6,2,3,7,3,7,8,2,8,7,3,9,5,4,10,11,3,6,
%T A401192 12,4,12,13,3,8,14,5,14,8,5,15,11,6,9,17,5,17,18,3,18,19,7,19,11,6,13,
%U A401192 17,7,13,22,8,22,16,5,23,24,8,17,14,7,25,26,8,15
%N A401192 a(n) is the number of inequivalent lattice triangles containing n lattice points, with no boundary lattice points other than their three vertices.
%C A401192 Two triangles are equivalent under affine unimodular transformations x -> A*x + b, where A is in GL_2(Z) and b is in Z^2. Reflections are allowed.
%C A401192 This sequence is the right edge T(n, n - 3) of A401110. The enumeration of these clean lattice triangles was established by Khan and Khan (Theorem 17; see link).
%C A401192 By Pick's theorem, the doubled area is m = 2*n - 5. Every such triangle is equivalent to one with vertices (0, 0), (1, 0), and (p, m). The shear (x, y) -> (x + t*y, y), with t in Z, replaces p by p + t*m, so only p modulo m matters. The other two edges are primitive iff gcd(p, m) = gcd(p - 1, m) = 1. The number of admissible residues is A058026(m). Relabeling the vertices induces an action of S_3 on these residues. The identity fixes A058026(m) residues, each of the three transpositions fixes one residue, and each of the two 3-cycles fixes A000086(m) residues. Burnside's lemma gives the formula below.
%H A401192 Felix Huber, <a href="/A401192/b401192.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 3..10000</a>.
%H A401192 Felix Huber, <a href="/A401192/a401192.pdf">Why is a(6) = 2?</a>.
%H A401192 Mizan R. Khan and Riaz R. Khan, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.11081">To count clean triangles we count on imph(n)</a>, arXiv:2012.11081 [math.CO], 2020.
%H A401192 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Cauchy-FrobeniusLemma.html">Cauchy-Frobenius Lemma</a>.
%H A401192 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/PicksTheorem.html">Pick's Theorem</a>.
%F A401192 a(n) = (A058026(2*n - 5) + 3 + 2*A000086(2*n - 5))/6.
%F A401192 If p = 2*n - 5 is prime and p > 3, then a(n) = (p + 5)/6 if p == 1 (mod 6), and a(n) = (p + 1)/6 if p == 5 (mod 6).
%F A401192 Sum_{k = 3 .. n} a(k) ~ c*n^2/3, where c = Product_{p prime} (1 - 2/p^2) = A065474.
%e A401192 a(6) = 2: There are two inequivalent lattice triangles with six lattice points, three of them interior. The doubled area is m = 7. The admissible residues are p = 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 (mod 7), which form two orbits under vertex permutations: {2, 4, 6} and {3, 5}. Hence a(6) = 2. Equivalently, A058026(7) = 5 and A000086(7) = 2, giving a(6) = (5 + 3 + 2*2)/6 = 2.
%p A401192 A401192 := proc(n)
%p A401192     option remember;
%p A401192     local e, m, q, t, u, v;
%p A401192     m := 2*n - 5;
%p A401192     u := 1;
%p A401192     v := 1;
%p A401192     for t in ifactors(m)[2] do
%p A401192         q := t[1];
%p A401192         e := t[2];
%p A401192         u := u*q^(e - 1)*(q - 2);
%p A401192         if q = 3 then
%p A401192             if e > 1 then
%p A401192                 v := 0;
%p A401192             end if;
%p A401192         elif irem(q, 3) = 1 then
%p A401192             v := 2*v;
%p A401192         else
%p A401192             v := 0;
%p A401192         end if;
%p A401192     end do;
%p A401192     (u + 3 + 2*v)/6;
%p A401192 end proc:
%p A401192 seq(A401192(n), n = 3 .. 80);
%Y A401192 Cf. A000086, A034017, A058026, A065474, A070554, A372217, A372218, A401110.
%K A401192 nonn,easy,new
%O A401192 3,4
%A A401192 _Felix Huber_, Oct 19 2026
